Does replication prefault actually help, and what lookahead is right?

Disabled on purpose: this is an evaluation harness, not a regression test.  It
takes minutes and needs a cluster.

The test Makefile matches directories named <name>.test, so rename it to run:

    cd tests
    mv repprefault_perf.test.disabled repprefault_perf.test
    make repprefault_perf          # with your usual clustered setup
    mv repprefault_perf.test repprefault_perf.test.disabled

ROWS, PASSES and LOOKAHEADS are environment overrides if the defaults do not
put enough pressure on the buffer pool.

LOOKAHEADS entries: 'off' disables prefault, a number is a static lookahead
(adaptive control off), and 'adapt' runs the adaptive controller.  Run 'adapt'
twice in a row -- the first phase spends part of its time converging from the
default budget, the second measures the converged state.  Each adapt row also
prints the budget and thread count the phase ended at; compare the second
adapt row against the best static row to see what the controller found on its
own.  Keep adapt phases after the static rows: the controller only grows the
thread pool, and a static phase run after it would inherit the bigger pool.

The regular repprefault.test proves the machinery works.  It cannot say whether
the machinery is worth anything, because its workload is the worst possible
case for prefault: pure inserts into a fresh table, so ~96% of the pages a log
record names do not exist on disk yet, and the working set fits in cache so the
apply thread never waits for I/O anyway.

This harness builds the opposite workload.  It loads a table several times the
size of the replicant's cache (see lrl.options), then drives scattered UPDATEs
against rows that already exist.

The payload column is an inline char(2000), not a blob, and it is filled with
hex(randomblob(1000)) rather than a short string.  Both details matter: comdb2
stores blobs in a separate file, so updating an int column on a blob table
touches only the tiny data pages, and a char(2000) holding 'x' is stored short.
Get either wrong and the working set is under a megabyte.  Those read-modify-write pages that are really
on disk and really evicted, which is the only situation prefault can help.

It then sweeps rep_prefault_lookahead and prints one row per setting.  Read it
top to bottom:

  secs          wall time of the master's updates only, not the drain wait.
  apply_miss    page faults taken by the replication apply thread.  This is the
                thing prefault exists to remove.  If it does not fall as the
                lookahead rises, prefault is not helping and nothing else in
                the table matters.
  pf_in         prefaults that did real I/O.
  pf_late       prefaults that found the page already cached, so did nothing.
                If this is most of them, the working set is fitting in cache
                and the run cannot tell you anything -- shrink cachekb.
  ready         prefaulted pages that were already in memory when apply reached
                them -- prefault won the race.
  inflight      apply arrived while the prefault read was still running.  It
                still waited, just less.  Rising means the lookahead is short.
  evict_unused  prefaulted, never used, evicted.  Rising means the lookahead
                reaches further than the cache can hold, and the reads are
                being thrown away.

The useful lookahead is the largest one where inflight is small and
evict_unused has not started to climb.  If both stay at zero across the whole
sweep, the workload still is not stressing the buffer pool -- shrink cachekb in
lrl.options or grow ROWS in runit and try again.  Note that lowering cachekb
also means lowering cachekbmin, or the clamp puts it straight back up to 64MB;
check the effective size with

    select value from comdb2_tunables where name='cachekb'

'off' is measured twice, first and last.  Those two rows should be close.  If
they are not, the sweep is drifting -- most likely the phases are not returning
to the same cache state -- and the settings between them cannot be compared.

Findings from the o1/o2/o3 sweep (Aug 2026), for whoever runs this next:

The counters obey two identities, and checking them tells you whether a run is
trustworthy: pf_in + pf_late = the off-row apply_miss (every needed page gets
requested exactly once), and apply_miss = pf_late + evict_unused (every
residual miss is a request that ran after apply already faulted the page, or a
page read too early and evicted).  If they do not close, something new is
leaking pages past the parser.

pf_late is the pool-capacity signal, not a lookahead signal: it climbs when
requests sit in the pool queue until they are stale.  8 threads saturated on
this hardware; 32 was past the knee.  The pool can be resized live with
    exec procedure sys.cmd.send("repprefault maxt N")
On this hardware the parallel path measured best at lookahead 8 with 32
threads (54k misses down to 9.2k); the serial path plateaus at 16-64.  The
shipped defaults (16/32) sit near both optima.
